\title{Chromatic Counting Functions:\\Deletion--Contraction, Extremal Evaluations, and Component Factorization}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a finite simple graph $G$ and a nonnegative integer $q$, let $P(G,q)$ be the number of proper vertex colorings of $G$ by $q$ colors. We give a self-contained development of its basic counting theory. We prove the formulas $P(E_n,q)=q^n$ and $P(K_n,q)=\fall qn$. We define edge deletion and a concrete simple-graph contraction, then partition the proper colorings after deletion according to whether the endpoints of the deleted edge receive distinct or equal colors. An explicit restriction-and-extension bijection identifies the equal-color class with the proper colorings of the contraction. This yields
\[
P(G-e,q)=P(G,q)+P(G/e,q).
\]
We also prove multiplicativity under disjoint union, $P(G\sqcup H,q)=P(G,q)P(H,q)$, and derive a bound on the contraction contribution. These results provide an exact recursive algorithm for chromatic counts. We discuss exhaustive enumeration, deletion--contraction, component-aware evaluation, complexity, applications, and the distinction between the established counting theory and broader goals involving polynomial coefficients, planar coloring, maximum-degree bounds, and basis positivity.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Graph coloring models assignments subject to pairwise incompatibility. A vertex may represent a region, task, transmitter, register candidate, or experimental condition; an edge records that its endpoints may not receive the same label. The familiar feasibility question asks whether a graph can be colored from a palette of a given size. The enumerative question asks how many such colorings exist.

The count distinguishes a rigid instance from a flexible one even when both require the same minimum number of colors. It also supports sensitivity analysis: one may ask exactly how many assignments are gained by removing a constraint, or how independent subsystems combine.

The fundamental relation is deletion--contraction. Given an edge $e$, delete it. Every proper coloring of the resulting graph either still separates the endpoints or gives them the same color. The first class is precisely the set of proper colorings before deletion. The second is naturally equivalent to the colorings of the graph in which the endpoints are merged. The recurrence follows by cardinality.

Our treatment emphasizes finite sets and explicit bijections. This keeps the argument valid for every nonnegative palette size, including $q=0$, and clarifies the exact role of contraction. Although $P(G,q)$ anticipates the chromatic polynomial, the results require only its counting interpretation at natural values. Constructing an integer-coefficient polynomial and proving polynomial identities is a further algebraic step.

\section{Definitions and conventions}

\begin{definition}[Finite simple graph]
A finite simple graph $G=(V,E)$ consists of a finite vertex set $V$ and an edge set of unordered two-element subsets of $V$. Equivalently, adjacency is a symmetric, irreflexive relation. We write $x\sim_G y$ when $x$ and $y$ are adjacent. The edgeless graph $E_n$ has $n$ vertices and no edges; the complete graph $K_n$ joins every pair of distinct vertices.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proper coloring and chromatic count]
For a finite color set $C$, a proper $C$-coloring is a function $c:V\to C$ satisfying
\[
x\sim_G y\implies c(x)\ne c(y).
\]
For a palette of cardinality $q$, define
\[
P(G,q)=\#\{c:V\to\{1,\ldots,q\}:c\text{ is proper}\}.
\]
The value depends only on $q$, not on the color names.
\end{definition}

For $q=0$, finite-set conventions apply: there is one function from the empty vertex set to the empty palette and none from a nonempty set.

\begin{definition}[Falling factorial]
For $q,n\ge0$, let
\[
\fall qn=q(q-1)\cdots(q-n+1),\qquad \fall q0=1.
\]
It counts injections from an $n$-element set into a $q$-element set and is zero when $n>q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Deletion]
If $e=\{a,b\}$ is an edge of $G$, the deletion $G-e$ retains every vertex and every edge except $e$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Contraction]
The contraction $G/e$ merges $a$ and $b$. Concretely, retain every vertex other than $b$ and use $a$ as the merged representative. Distinct retained vertices $x,y$ are adjacent if they were adjacent in $G$, or if one is $a$ and the other was adjacent to $b$. Duplicate edges have no effect and loops are excluded.
\end{definition}

The construction redirects every incidence of $b$ to $a$ and is isomorphic to the usual quotient identifying the endpoints. Since $e$ is an edge, $a\ne b$.

\begin{definition}[Disjoint union]
For graphs $G=(V,E_G)$ and $H=(W,E_H)$ on disjoint vertex sets, $G\sqcup H$ has vertex set $V\sqcup W$, inherits adjacency within each summand, and has no cross edges.
\end{definition}

\section{Extremal evaluations}

\begin{theorem}[Empty-Graph Formula]
For every $n,q\ge0$,
\[
P(E_n,q)=q^n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The graph has no adjacency constraints, so every function from its $n$ vertices to the $q$ colors is proper. There are $q^n$ such functions. This includes $n=0$, where the unique empty function gives $q^0=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete-Graph Formula]
For every $n,q\ge0$,
\[
P(K_n,q)=\fall qn.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every two distinct vertices are adjacent, so a coloring is proper exactly when it is injective. After ordering the vertices, their images may be selected in $q,q-1,\ldots,q-n+1$ ways. If $n>q$, no injection exists and the count is zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $q\ge n$, $P(K_n,q)=q!/(q-n)!$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The edge partition}
Fix a graph $G$, an edge $e=\{a,b\}$, and a finite palette $C$. Write $\mathcal C(G,C)$ for the set of proper $C$-colorings. Partition $\mathcal C(G-e,C)$ into
\[
\mathcal C_{\ne}=\{c:c(a)\ne c(b)\},\qquad
\mathcal C_{=}=\{c:c(a)=c(b)\}.
\]
These classes are disjoint and exhaustive.

\begin{lemma}[Distinct-Endpoint Class]
The class $\mathcal C_{\ne}$ is exactly $\mathcal C(G,C)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A proper coloring of $G$ remains proper after deleting $e$ and separates its adjacent endpoints. Conversely, a proper coloring of $G-e$ that separates $a$ and $b$ satisfies the sole constraint omitted during deletion and hence every constraint of $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Equal-Endpoint Contraction Bijection]
There is a bijection
\[
\mathcal C_{=}\cong\mathcal C(G/e,C).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Given $c\in\mathcal C_{=}$, restrict it to the retained vertices, omitting $b$. Ordinary retained edges remain properly colored. If a contraction edge arises by redirecting an edge incident to $b$, its other endpoint differs in color from $c(b)=c(a)$, so the restriction is proper.

Conversely, extend a proper coloring $d$ of $G/e$ by assigning $d(a)$ to both $a$ and $b$. Edges not incident to $b$ are respected because they appear in the contraction. Every surviving edge from $b$ is represented by an edge from its other endpoint to $a$, so it too is respected. Restriction followed by extension recovers $c$ because $c(a)=c(b)$; extension followed by restriction recovers $d$. Thus the maps are inverse.
\end{proof}

\section{Deletion--contraction}

\begin{theorem}[Deletion--Contraction]
For every finite simple graph $G$, every edge $e$ of $G$, and every $q\ge0$,
\[
P(G-e,q)=P(G,q)+P(G/e,q).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Partition the proper colorings of $G-e$ by equality of the endpoint colors. The distinct class is the set of proper colorings of $G$, and the equal class is bijective with the proper colorings of $G/e$. Their cardinalities add.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Subtractive Form]
Under the same assumptions,
\[
P(G,q)=P(G-e,q)-P(G/e,q).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Rearrange the preceding equality in the natural numbers.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Contraction Bound]
Under the same assumptions,
\[
P(G/e,q)\le P(G-e,q).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The deletion count is the contraction count plus the nonnegative count $P(G,q)$. Equivalently, contraction colorings correspond to the equal-endpoint subset of deletion colorings.
\end{proof}

Thus $P(G/e,q)$ is the exact increase in feasible assignments caused by deleting $e$:
\[
P(G-e,q)-P(G,q)=P(G/e,q).
\]

\section{Disjoint-union multiplicativity}

\begin{theorem}[Disjoint-Union Product Formula]
For finite simple graphs $G,H$ and every $q\ge0$,
\[
P(G\sqcup H,q)=P(G,q)P(H,q).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Restrict a coloring of the union to each summand. Both restrictions are proper. Conversely, combine any proper coloring of $G$ with any proper coloring of $H$; every edge lies within one summand. Restriction and combination are inverse, giving a bijection with the Cartesian product of the two coloring sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Finite Component Product]
If $G$ is the disjoint union of $G_1,\ldots,G_r$, then
\[
P(G,q)=\prod_{i=1}^r P(G_i,q).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $r$ using the binary product formula.
\end{proof}

\section{Worked examples}

\begin{example}[Three-vertex path]
Let $P_3$ have edges $uv$ and $vw$. Choosing the middle color and then independently choosing a different color for each endpoint gives
\[
P(P_3,q)=q(q-1)^2.
\]
Alternatively, $P_3=K_3-e$ and $K_3/e\cong K_2$, so
\[
P(P_3,q)=P(K_3,q)+P(K_2,q)
=q(q-1)(q-2)+q(q-1)=q(q-1)^2.
\]
At $q=3$, the identity is $12=6+6$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[An edge and an isolated vertex]
For $G=K_2\sqcup E_1$,
\[
P(G,q)=q(q-1)q=q^2(q-1),
\]
which equals $18$ at $q=3$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Four-cycle]
A direct computation for $C_4$ gives
\[
P(C_4,q)=(q-1)^4+(q-1).
\]
At $q=3$, the count is $18$. This is an illustrative computation, not a general cycle theorem here; it follows by deletion--contraction and path simplification or by sequential conditioning.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Exhaustive enumeration}
Enumerate all $q^n$ functions from an $n$-vertex set to the palette and reject any assignment having a monochromatic edge. With $m$ edges, worst-case time is $O(mq^n)$ and coloring storage is $O(n)$. Early rejection helps in practice but does not change the worst-case exponent. This method directly implements the definition and gives a useful reference for small graphs.

\subsection{Deletion--contraction evaluation}
An exact recursive evaluator proceeds as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If the graph has no edges, return $q^n$.
\item If it has several connected components, evaluate each and multiply.
\item Choose an edge $e$.
\item Return the value for $G-e$ minus the value for $G/e$.
\end{enumerate}
The recursion is exponential in the worst case. Deletion lowers the edge count and contraction lowers the vertex count, ensuring termination under a suitable well-founded size measure. Edge choice changes the recursion tree but not the answer. High-degree edge selection, early component splitting, canonicalization, and memoization can greatly reduce repeated work.

\subsection{Partition diagnostics}
For a selected edge, enumerate the deletion colorings and count the endpoint-distinct and endpoint-equal classes. The theorem predicts
\[
N_{\ne}=P(G,q),\qquad N_{=}=P(G/e,q),\qquad
N_{\ne}+N_{=}=P(G-e,q).
\]
This diagnostic exposes the bijective mechanism rather than merely comparing final totals.

\section{Applications}
In scheduling, vertices are events, edges are conflicts, and colors are time slots. The count measures the number of feasible schedules. In frequency assignment, adjacent transmitters must use different channels, and disconnected systems factor independently. In register allocation, overlapping live ranges are adjacent and colors are registers; a large count indicates flexibility for secondary optimization.

For constraint sensitivity, the exact marginal effect of removing an edge is
\[
P(G/e,q)=P(G-e,q)-P(G,q).
\]
Ranking edges by this quantity identifies constraints whose relaxation unlocks the largest number of assignments.

\section{Scope and limitations}
The results concern natural-number evaluations. A polynomial-level theorem requires constructing an element of $\mathbb Z[X]$ whose evaluations recover $P(G,q)$ and lifting deletion--contraction to polynomial equality. The recurrence motivates this construction but is not identical to it.

The Four Color Theorem additionally requires a precise planar embedding theory and a proof that every planar graph is four-colorable. Brooks' theorem is a structural existence result involving maximum degree, complete graphs, and odd cycles. Positivity for claw-free graphs requires a specified basis and richer symmetric or quasisymmetric polynomial infrastructure. None of these statements follows from the elementary recurrence alone, and they are not claimed here.

\section{Future directions}
The first continuation is to construct the integer chromatic polynomial and prove evaluation agreement, degree, leading coefficient, and coefficient sign alternation. Further operation laws should cover joins, vertex sums, forests, cycles, and complete multipartite graphs. The disjoint-union theorem suggests systematic factorization over connected components.

Expansions in falling-factorial and tree-related bases may reveal further combinatorics. Claw-free positivity requires fixing the intended $T$-basis and building its algebraic setting. Complete treatments of Brooks' theorem and planar four-colorability require structural methods beyond counting.

Algorithmically, memoized deletion--contraction can be improved through graph canonicalization, separator detection, component splitting, and edge-selection heuristics. Comparison with exhaustive enumeration can identify graph families best suited to each method.

\section{Conclusion}
Chromatic counting acquires its power from a simple partition. For $e=\{a,b\}$, proper colorings after deletion divide according to whether $a$ and $b$ differ or coincide. The distinct class recovers $G$; the equal class is bijective with $G/e$. Therefore
\[
P(G-e,q)=P(G,q)+P(G/e,q).
\]
Together with $P(E_n,q)=q^n$, $P(K_n,q)=\fall qn$, and multiplicativity on disjoint unions, this gives a coherent calculus for exact chromatic counts. The proofs are finite and bijective: global coloring behavior is resolved by asking, at one edge, whether two colors differ or agree.

